/// Behavioral API documentation for the BK PERFORMANCE PRO backend.
///
/// Static Markdown authored from the current backend source. It reads no
/// runtime state, so it is safe to serve from a query.

mixin () {
  /// Return the backend's API documentation as Markdown.
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# BK PERFORMANCE PRO - Backend API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe backend stores, per signed-in user, three independent collections: games, optimization profiles and sensitivity profiles. It also exposes a stateless Free Fire sensitivity generator. All persistent data is keyed by the caller's principal, so one user can never read or mutate another user's records.\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\nEvery mutating method and every per-user read requires a signed (non-anonymous) caller. The caller's principal is taken from the IC message, never from a parameter, so a caller cannot act on behalf of another principal.\n\nThe frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\n## Authorization\n\n- `listGames`, `createGame`, `updateGame`, `deleteGame`\n- `listOptimizationProfiles`, `saveOptimizationProfile`, `applyOptimizationProfile`, `restoreOptimizationProfile`, `deleteOptimizationProfile`\n- `listSensitivityProfiles`, `saveSensitivityProfile`, `updateSensitivityProfile`, `deleteSensitivityProfile`\n\nThese operate only on the caller's own records. A record that belongs to another principal is invisible: reads return nothing for it and mutations report not-found rather than leaking its existence.\n\n`generateSensitivity` is a stateless query and requires no authentication; it reads no stored data.\n\nRole management (`getCallerUserRole`, `isCallerAdmin`, `assignCallerUserRole`, `_initialize_access_control`) comes from the shared authorization component.\n\n### Registration prerequisite for direct API callers\n\nRegistration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend, so a principal that never did so is unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered. A direct API caller must therefore register itself before invoking any role-guarded endpoint, including guarded queries:\n\n1. Authenticate as a signed (non-anonymous) caller.\n2. Call `_initialize_access_control()` once. The first caller to invoke it becomes the initial `#admin`; every later caller receives the default `#user` role. Calling it again is a no-op for an already-registered principal.\n3. Only then call `getCallerUserRole`, `isCallerAdmin`, `assignCallerUserRole`, or any other role-guarded endpoint.\n\nAn anonymous caller is never registered: `_initialize_access_control()` silently ignores it, and `getCallerUserRole()` returns `#guest` while `isCallerAdmin()` returns `false`.\n\n### Unregistered caller outcome\n\nA signed-in caller that has not registered traps with the exact message `User is not registered` on any endpoint that resolves its role, including the guarded query `getCallerUserRole()` and `isCallerAdmin()`. The trap rolls back the message and reaches the caller as an opaque reject, so register first. `assignCallerUserRole` additionally traps with `Unauthorized: Only admins can assign user roles` when the caller is not an admin.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- Timestamps (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`) are signed `Int` nanoseconds from `Time.now()`.\n- Identifiers (`id`, `gameId`) are `Nat`.\n- Sensitivity values are `Nat` in the inclusive range 0..200.\n- `dpi` is `?Nat`: `null` means no DPI number applies.\n- Variants are Candid variants: `SensitivityType` = `low | medium | high | custom`; `DpiMode` = `current | recommended | withoutDpi`; `PlayStyle` = `dragScope | precision | closeCombat | balanced | custom`; `FingerMode` = `two | three | four | custom`.\n\n## Sensitivity generation\n\n`generateSensitivity(request)` is deterministic and side-effect free: the same request always returns the same six values. It derives an anchor from the requested `sensitivityType` (low = 62, medium = 82, high = 104, custom = 82) and applies fixed per-axis offsets (Geral +0, Ponto vermelho -6, Mira 2x -10, Mira 4x -16, Mira AWM -22, Olhadinha +14), clamping every value into 0..200.\n\nDPI handling follows `dpiMode`:\n\n- `recommended` returns the recommended DPI 480.\n- `current` returns the `dpi` the caller supplied, or `null` when none was supplied. The backend never invents a DPI the caller did not provide.\n- `withoutDpi` returns `null`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\nAll methods are single-message calls with no asynchronous continuation: a response means the operation completed. There is nothing to poll.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- `saveSensitivityProfile` (and `saveOptimizationProfile`) always creates a new record with a fresh id; retrying a save after a timeout creates a duplicate rather than updating the first record.\n- `updateSensitivityProfile` and `deleteSensitivityProfile` are idempotent in effect: updating a missing record returns `null`, and deleting a missing record returns `false`.\n- `deleteSensitivityProfile` is destructive and cannot be undone.\n\n## Errors and gotchas\n\n- `updateSensitivityProfile` returns `?SensitivityProfile`; `null` means the record does not exist for this caller.\n- `deleteSensitivityProfile` returns `Bool`; `false` means nothing was deleted.\n- `generateSensitivity` is a query: it never persists its result. Saving a generated sensitivity is a separate `saveSensitivityProfile` call.\n- The generator does not discover a device's real DPI; it only echoes a DPI the caller supplied or returns the fixed recommendation.\n";
  };
};
